Make DECD's TVTOP client registration optional, at compile time.

DECD's probe ends with sunxi_tvtop_client_register(), which returns
-EPROBE_DEFER when TVTOP has not probed, so DECD defers forever without
it. That call is pure bookkeeping: its entire body is a
device_link_add(client, tvtop, DL_FLAG_AUTOREMOVE_CONSUMER) for probe and
suspend ordering. It touches no hardware.

Enabling TVTOP purely to satisfy it is the wrong trade for a first
bring-up. sunxi_tvtop's probe owns CLK_PANEL, CLK_DEINT, CLK_SVP_DTL,
CLK_BUS_DISP and RST_BUS_DISP -- exactly the display resources U-Boot
programs before Linux starts, and which the display depends on surviving
the handoff (docs/claude-display-handoff.md item 4). Bringing up a second
owner of those clocks in the same session that first enables DECD would
confound any failure between the two.

The guard is at COMPILE time, not just at run time. Leaving the call in
place and merely ignoring its return still emits an undefined reference to
an EXPORT_SYMBOL owned by the tvtop module, so depmod would record the
dependency and `modprobe sunxi-decd` would silently load TVTOP anyway --
exactly what this is trying to avoid. With CONFIG_SUNXI_TVTOP=n the
declaration becomes a static inline stub and no symbol reference is
emitted at all.

This is deliberately narrow, and does NOT make TVTOP unnecessary in
general: the vendor ships both, and a full display stack wants the real
resource ownership TVTOP provides. It makes DECD independently testable
first. Milestone 4's "enable TVTOP first, then DECD" assumes both work;
this allows the harder one to be isolated when neither has ever run.

--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_core.c	2026-08-12 00:34:11.698113636 -0700
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_core.c	2026-08-12 00:34:11.707155554 -0700
@@ -191,15 +191,20 @@
 		goto err_dev;
 	dec->attr_group = (struct attribute_group *)&dec_attr_group;
 	pm_runtime_enable(&pdev->dev);
+	/*
+	 * Best-effort: this only adds a device_link for probe/suspend
+	 * ordering and touches no hardware. Without TVTOP there is nothing
+	 * to order against, so failure here is not fatal.
+	 */
 	ret = sunxi_tvtop_client_register(&pdev->dev);
 	if (ret)
-		goto err_pm;
+		dev_info(&pdev->dev,
+			 "tvtop link unavailable (%d); continuing unordered\n",
+			 ret);
 	g_dec = dec;
 	of_platform_populate(pdev->dev.of_node, NULL, NULL, &pdev->dev);
 	dev_info(&pdev->dev, "reconstructed decd probed\n");
 	return 0;
-err_pm:
-	pm_runtime_disable(&pdev->dev);
 err_dev:
 	if (dec->attr_group)
 		sysfs_remove_group(&pdev->dev.kobj, dec->attr_group);
--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_types.h	2026-08-12 00:34:11.698089381 -0700
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_types.h	2026-08-12 00:34:11.707114387 -0700
@@ -33,7 +33,24 @@
 #define DECD_IOC_GET_VSYNC_TS      0x8008640au
 #define DECD_IOC_MAP_LINEAR_BUFFER 0xc010640bu
 
+/*
+ * Provided by the sunxi_tvtop module. The stub keeps DECD buildable and
+ * loadable without TVTOP: the real function only adds a device_link for
+ * probe/suspend ordering, so its absence costs ordering and nothing else.
+ *
+ * A compile-time stub rather than a runtime check, so no undefined reference
+ * to the tvtop EXPORT_SYMBOL is emitted -- otherwise depmod records a
+ * dependency and modprobe pulls TVTOP in regardless, which is what this
+ * avoids.
+ */
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SUNXI_TVTOP)
 extern int sunxi_tvtop_client_register(struct device *dev);
+#else
+static inline int sunxi_tvtop_client_register(struct device *dev)
+{
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
+#endif
 long dec_ioctl_file(struct file *file, unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg);
 
 struct dec_device;
